### Worktree cleanup

**You own the disk and the safety gate.** Prune merged or abandoned git worktrees to reclaim space. Deletion is irreversible, so every step guards against deleting something in use or holding uncommitted work.

1. Snapshot and audit. Record `df -h /`, then run `scripts/worktree-audit.sh` ([build the lever](../../build-the-lever/SKILL.md)). Reads paths from `git worktree list`, never hand-typed, since a hand-typed `myrepo-worktrees/x` misses one under another worktree root ([encode lessons in structure](../../encode-lessons-in-structure/SKILL.md)). Classifies each worktree by size, age, merge state, uncommitted work, PR state, newest chat that touched it, suggests a bucket. The transcript scan is slow, so background it.
2. The bucket is advice, not permission. The pinned and active chats are the real artifact ([prove it works](../../prove-it-works/SKILL.md)). Get that set from the user or sidebar, cross-check every candidate. The lever has marked `safe` a worktree the user had pinned, so the pinned set wins.
3. Verify usage before deleting. Every `verify-recent-chat` row, or anything you doubt: fan subagents out to read the transcripts and report whether the chat is pinned or ongoing and which worktrees it touches ([context window guard](../../context-window-guard/SKILL.md), transcripts are bulk). A pinned chat spawns [arena](../../arena/SKILL.md) and repro trees into sibling worktrees via background subagents, in use even when their names never hit the sidebar.
4. Pause on irreversible loss. `wip:N` = N tracked uncommitted edits. Show the diff, get a decision first: removing a clean worktree is recoverable from its branch, uncommitted work is gone. `scratch:N` = untracked throwaway, safe to drop, name the files. Per Autonomy, clean and merged and not-in-use proceeds. `wip` and in-use pause.
5. Prune the confirmed set. Per path, `git worktree remove --force <path>`. Dir survives on ignored build artifacts? `rm -rf` it, then `git worktree prune`. Branch refs survive, so no commits lost. Confirm with `df -h /` and re-list.
6. Other reclaimers. More when needed: editor caches, package caches (pnpm, uv, brew, yarn), build output the repository can regenerate. Clear only caches the user has not said to keep.

This is the one playbook that deletes user state with no code review to catch a slip, so the gates above are the review.

**Reply:** `df -h /` before and after with space reclaimed, the worktrees pruned, one-line reason for each held back (in-use by which chat, or uncommitted work).
